---
/**
 * Child Tax Credit Calculator — 미국 영어판 제도 도구(basis "law"). 한국 자녀장려금(가구 소득·재산 요건, 환급형)과
 * 제도가 달라 짝(hreflang)을 맺지 않는다(조사표 26행 미국 칸 '제도').
 * 계산은 lib/us/child-tax-credit.ts, 화면은 lib/us/child-tax-credit-ui.ts, 숫자는 rates/us/child-tax-credit-2026.json
 * (26 U.S.C. 24 · Rev. Proc. 2024-40·2025-32 §3.05 · Schedule 8812 (2025)·지침 · IRS CTC 페이지 — 2026-10-23 확인).
 *
 * 소제목은 미국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/us-child-tax-credit-2026-10-23.csv):
 *   child tax credit calculator 2026 with dependents·based on income·high income / child tax credit 2026 amount·income limit·
 *   phase out·age limit·for 17 year olds·vs 2025 / additional child tax credit refundable·vs child tax credit /
 *   schedule 8812 credit limit worksheet a
 * 쓰지 않는 것(원문 미확인·주의 원칙): 환급 날짜, 2021 월 선지급(advance·monthly payment), 신생아 특례(newborn),
 * 주(州) credit(california·nj·texas), Worksheet B·Form 2555·자영업세, 자격 판정(원문 안내만).
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { amounts, computeCtc, phaseoutEnd, ODC, THRESHOLD, EARNED_FLOOR } from "../../../lib/us/child-tax-credit";
import { usd0 } from "../../../lib/us/fica";
import "../../../styles/global.css";

const title = "Child Tax Credit Calculator 2026 — IRS Schedule 8812, Refundable ACTC";
const description =
  "Estimate the federal child tax credit for 2025 and 2026: $2,200 per child, up to $1,700 refundable, the $500 other-dependent credit and the $200,000/$400,000 phase-out.";

const a26 = amounts(2026);
const first = computeCtc({ year: 2026, status: "other", children: 2, otherDependents: 0, magi: 40_000, taxLimit: 1500, earned: 40_000 });
const firstHint = `Line 14 ${usd0(first.line14)} reduces your tax; line 27 ${usd0(first.line27)} is refundable (15% of earned income over $2,500 is ${usd0(first.line20)}, the cap is ${usd0(first.line16b)}). 2026 uses Rev. Proc. 2025-32 amounts with the 2025 Schedule 8812 method.`;

const hi = computeCtc({ year: 2026, status: "mfj", children: 2, otherDependents: 0, magi: 410_500, taxLimit: 100_000, earned: 410_500 });
const low = computeCtc({ year: 2026, status: "other", children: 1, otherDependents: 0, magi: 10_000, taxLimit: 0, earned: 10_000 });
const part = computeCtc({ year: 2026, status: "other", children: 1, otherDependents: 0, magi: 30_000, taxLimit: 600, earned: 30_000 });
---

<html lang="en-US">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Child Tax Credit Calculator</h1>
      <p>
        The federal child tax credit is {usd0(a26.ctcPerChild)} per qualifying child for 2025 and 2026, plus {usd0(ODC)} for each other
        dependent. It first reduces the tax you owe; if the credit is bigger than your tax, up to {usd0(a26.actcCapPerChild)} per child can come
        back as a refund — the additional child tax credit. This calculator follows Schedule 8812 line by line. Enter how many children
        and other dependents you claim, your modified AGI, your tax before credits and your earned income.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-year">Tax year</label>
          <select id="ct-year" data-ui="off">
            <option value="2026" selected>2026 (filed in 2027)</option>
            <option value="2025">2025 (filed in 2026)</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-status">Filing status</label>
          <select id="ct-status" data-ui="off">
            <option value="other" selected>Single, head of household, married filing separately or qualifying surviving spouse</option>
            <option value="mfj">Married filing jointly</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-kids">Qualifying children under 17 with an SSN</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="ct-kids" value="2" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-other">Other dependents ($500 each)</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="ct-other" value="0" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-magi">Modified AGI ($) — usually Form 1040 line 11</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="ct-magi" value="40,000" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-tax">Tax before credits ($) — Credit Limit Worksheet A</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="ct-tax" value="1,500" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ct-earned">Earned income ($) — leave blank if the same as MAGI</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="ct-earned" value="" placeholder="same as MAGI" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap" id="ct-ss-wrap" hidden>
          <label for="ct-ss">3+ children: Social Security and Medicare withheld ($) — W-2 boxes 4 and 6</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="ct-ss" value="" placeholder="0" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap" id="ct-eic-wrap" hidden>
          <label for="ct-eic">3+ children: earned income credit ($) — Form 1040 line 27a</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="ct-eic" value="" placeholder="0" autocomplete="off" data-ui="off" />
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value" data-bar="out-nonref,out-actc,out-reduce">
          <p class="label">Child tax credit total</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">{usd0(first.total)}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="ct-hint">{firstHint}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Nonrefundable (CTC + ODC)</span>
            <span class="value" id="out-nonref">{usd0(first.line14)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Refundable (ACTC)</span>
            <span class="value" id="out-actc">{usd0(first.line27)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Income phase-out</span>
            <span class="value" id="out-reduce">{usd0(first.line11)}</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Child tax credit 2026 amount</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tax year</th>
              <th scope="col">Per qualifying child</th>
              <th scope="col">Refundable part, max per child</th>
              <th scope="col">Per other dependent</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">2026</th><td>{usd0(a26.ctcPerChild)}</td><td>{usd0(a26.actcCapPerChild)}</td><td>{usd0(ODC)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">2025</th><td>{usd0(amounts(2025).ctcPerChild)}</td><td>{usd0(amounts(2025).actcCapPerChild)}</td><td>{usd0(ODC)}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Child tax credit 2026 vs 2025:</strong> the amounts are the same. The law sets {usd0(a26.ctcPerChild)} for 2025 and indexes it
        for inflation after that, in steps of $100; Rev. Proc. 2025-32 kept 2026 at {usd0(a26.ctcPerChild)} and the refundable cap at
        {" "}{usd0(a26.actcCapPerChild)}. So the year menu above changes nothing yet. The 2026 Schedule 8812 is not out, so 2026 results use the 2026
        amounts with the 2025 form's method — compare with the 2026 form once it is published.
      </p>

      <h2>Child tax credit income limit and phase-out</h2>
      <p>
        The credit starts to shrink when modified AGI is over {usd0(THRESHOLD.mfj)} on a joint return or {usd0(THRESHOLD.other)} for any
        other filing status. It drops by $50 for each $1,000 — or part of $1,000 — above that line (Schedule 8812 lines 9–11). Because any
        part counts as a full $1,000, $425 over the limit already costs $50.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example: married filing jointly, two children, MAGI {usd0(410_500)}. The excess {usd0(10_500)} rounds up to {usd0(hi.line10)}, so the
        credit is {usd0(hi.line8)} − {usd0(hi.line11)} = {usd0(hi.line12)}. For a high income the credit is gone once MAGI is over
        {" "}{usd0(phaseoutEnd("other", a26.ctcPerChild))} (one child, single) or {usd0(phaseoutEnd("mfj", 2 * a26.ctcPerChild))} (two
        children, joint).
      </p>

      <h2>Additional child tax credit vs child tax credit</h2>
      <p>
        The child tax credit and the {usd0(ODC)} credit for other dependents only reduce tax. Schedule 8812 caps them at your tax from
        Credit Limit Worksheet A (line 14). Whatever is left over can become the <strong>additional child tax credit</strong>, which is
        refundable — but only for qualifying children, never for other dependents. It is the smallest of:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the unused credit (line 16a),</li>
        <li>{usd0(a26.actcCapPerChild)} × the number of qualifying children (line 16b), and</li>
        <li>15% of earned income above {usd0(EARNED_FLOOR)} (line 20).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Example: single, one child, earned income {usd0(30_000)}, tax {usd0(600)}. The credit uses {usd0(part.line14)} against tax, and the
        remaining {usd0(part.line16a)} is under the {usd0(part.line16b)} cap and under 15% × $27,500 = {usd0(part.line20)}, so all
        {" "}{usd0(part.line27)} is refunded — {usd0(part.total)} in total. With earned income of {usd0(10_000)} and no tax, 15% × $7,500 limits the
        refund to {usd0(low.line27)}.
      </p>
      <p>
        With three or more qualifying children there is a second test (Part II-B): if Social Security and Medicare tax withheld minus
        the earned income credit is larger than line 20, that amount is used instead. The calculator asks for those two numbers when you
        enter three or more children. It does not include self-employment tax or excess Social Security withholding, which also count in
        Part II-B.
      </p>

      <h2>Schedule 8812 Credit Limit Worksheet A</h2>
      <p>
        "Tax before credits" in the calculator is line 13 of Schedule 8812, from Credit Limit Worksheet A in the 2025 instructions: the
        tax on Form 1040 line 18, minus the credits on Schedule 3 lines 1, 2, 3, 4, 5b, 6d, 6f, 6l and 6m. If that amount is zero, the nonrefundable
        part is zero and only the refundable part is left. If you have a mortgage interest credit, adoption credit, residential clean
        energy credit or D.C. first-time homebuyer credit, the instructions add Worksheet B, which this calculator does not do.
      </p>

      <h2>Child tax credit age limit and eligibility</h2>
      <p>This calculator does not decide who qualifies. The main IRS rules for a qualifying child:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Under 17 at the end of the tax year. A 17-year-old does not qualify for the child tax credit but can count for the {usd0(ODC)} other-dependent credit.</li>
        <li>Meets the relationship test for a qualifying child under section 152(c).</li>
        <li>Lived with you for more than half the year and did not provide more than half of their own support.</li>
        <li>Claimed as your dependent, and a U.S. citizen, national or resident alien.</li>
        <li>
          Has a Social Security number valid for employment issued by the due date of the return. Starting with 2025 you need a valid SSN
          too — on a joint return, at least one spouse.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A child who doesn't meet the age or SSN rule may still count as an other dependent. If you file Form 2555 (foreign earned
        income), you can't take the additional child tax credit.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>What is modified AGI here?</summary>
        <p>
          For most people it is the AGI on Form 1040 line 11. Schedule 8812 adds back income excluded as a Puerto Rico resident or on
          Form 2555 or Form 4563.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does this include my state's child tax credit?</summary>
        <p>No. It covers the federal credit only. Several states have their own, with their own rules — check your state's tax agency.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why are some numbers shown with cents?</summary>
        <p>
          The calculator keeps cents. On the return you may round every amount to whole dollars — drop under 50 cents, round 50 to 99
          cents up — as long as you round all of them.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/individuals/child-tax-credit" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Child Tax Credit</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1040s8.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Schedule 8812 (Form 1040), 2025</a> and <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/i1040s8.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">its instructions, including Credit Limit Worksheet A</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rev. Proc. 2025-32 §3.05 (2026 amounts)</a> and <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-24-40.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rev. Proc. 2024-40 §3.05 (2025 refundable amount)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://uscode.house.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">26 U.S.C. 24</a> (Office of the Law Revision Counsel, release point Pub. L. 119-121)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Amounts checked on October 23, 2026. This is a calculator, not tax advice.</p>

      <AdSlot label="ctc-result-en-us" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <script src="../../../lib/us/child-tax-credit-ui.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
